# Ollama local model settings
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# Generation model — produces the actual draft/baseline answers.
OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3-vl:8b-instruct-q8_0

# Judge/grading model — used for ALL grading and correction roles (retrieval
# relevance, groundedness, usefulness, query rewriting, the correction editor,
# AND the external RAGAS evaluator). Deliberately kept separate from
# OLLAMA_MODEL: using the same model to both generate and grade its own
# output is a known source of self-evaluation bias, and a dense text-only
# instruct model is a better fit for strict classification/verification than
# a vision-language checkpoint. Pull with: ollama pull qwen2.5:14b-instruct
OLLAMA_JUDGE_MODEL=qwen2.5:14b-instruct

# --- VRAM sizing note ---
# qwen3-vl:8b-instruct-q8_0 (~8.5GB) + qwen2.5:14b-instruct default quant
# (~9GB) together need ~17.5GB VRAM to both stay resident at once. On a
# 12-16GB class GPU they will NOT both fit — Ollama will unload/reload
# between them every time the pipeline switches models (it switches twice
# per query: judge → generation → judge). This is real, measurable latency,
# not a bug: expect per-query latency to be noticeably higher than with a
# single shared model, especially during the graph/self-correction pipeline.
#
# If that swap cost is too high for your hardware/workflow, options in order
# of how much quality you give up:
#   1. Do nothing — accept the swap cost. It's a one-time cost per query, not
#      cumulative, and this is an offline eval script, not a live service.
#   2. Quantize the generator down (e.g. qwen3-vl:8b-instruct-q4_K_M, ~5GB)
#      to leave headroom for the judge to stay resident too.
#   3. Use a smaller judge that comfortably coexists with the generator, e.g.
#      OLLAMA_JUDGE_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b-instruct — still separate from the
#      generator (keeps the self-grading-bias fix), smaller quality jump over
#      the original 8B-VL judge, but no swap cost.
